          "term": "trefoil knot",
          "heading": false
        }
      ],
      "extract": "Torus knots are classified by a pair $(a,b)$ of coprime positive integers with $a>b.$ A knot is a torus knot if it lies on the surface of a torus, in which case the integers $a$ and $b$ count the number of times that the knot winds around the two cycles of the torus. The simplest example is the trefoil knot, which is the $(3,2)$-torus knot and has three crossings. In the standard knot catalogue notation it is $3_1,$",
      "extractCut": true
    },
